      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
